1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,188
Here's a genuinely confusing moment if you're not watching for it.

2
00:00:04,188 --> 00:00:06,573
A winning ad set's numbers start sliding.

3
00:00:06,573 --> 00:00:07,736
Click-through drops.

4
00:00:07,736 --> 00:00:09,539
Cost per acquisition creeps up.

5
00:00:09,539 --> 00:00:14,308
It looks exactly like the early warning signs Section 7.2's kill rule was built to

6
00:00:14,308 --> 00:00:14,657
catch.

7
00:00:14,657 --> 00:00:19,077
It isn't the same problem, and treating it like one wastes a genuine winner.

8
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:20,764
A losing ad set never worked.

9
00:00:20,764 --> 00:00:25,649
A fatigued ad set worked, then the same audience saw it enough times that it stopped

10
00:00:25,649 --> 00:00:26,114
landing.

11
00:00:26,114 --> 00:00:28,208
The fix for the first is killing it.

12
00:00:28,208 --> 00:00:30,534
The fix for the second is refreshing it.

13
00:00:30,534 --> 00:00:34,024
Why does this distinction matter enough for its own lecture?

14
00:00:34,024 --> 00:00:37,397
Because the wrong response is expensive in a specific way.

15
00:00:37,397 --> 00:00:42,166
Kill a genuine winner by mistake and you've thrown away the one angle that already

16
00:00:42,166 --> 00:00:44,783
proved itself, right when you needed it most.

17
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:49,552
Refresh a genuine loser instead of killing it, and you've spent effort dressing up

18
00:00:49,552 --> 00:00:53,332
an angle that was never going to work regardless of how it's cut.

19
00:00:53,332 --> 00:00:58,101
There's a practical fix for the moment of confusion itself: check frequency before

20
00:00:58,101 --> 00:00:59,265
you decide anything.

21
00:00:59,265 --> 00:01:04,150
It's sitting right there on the same dashboard that gave you the click-through drop.

22
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:08,861
You don't need a new tool, only a column you were probably already skimming past.

23
00:01:09,411 --> 00:01:12,350
Frequency is the tell that separates these two.

24
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:17,354
A never-worked loser dies early, before frequency has time to climb, because the

25
00:01:17,354 --> 00:01:18,667
kill rule catches it.

26
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:23,858
A fatigued winner has been running long enough, to the same audience, for frequency

27
00:01:23,858 --> 00:01:27,173
to climb well past where a fresh ad set usually sits.

28
00:01:27,173 --> 00:01:32,302
That's the actual signal worth watching, not just the declining click-through rate

29
00:01:32,302 --> 00:01:32,990
on its own.

30
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:37,117
Rising frequency plus declining performance, together, is fatigue.

31
00:01:37,117 --> 00:01:41,996
Declining performance with frequency that never climbed is a different problem

32
00:01:41,996 --> 00:01:44,935
entirely, worth investigating on its own terms.

33
00:01:44,935 --> 00:01:48,000
Think about why frequency specifically does this.

34
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,816
Every person in an audience only has so much genuine curiosity about the same

35
00:01:52,816 --> 00:01:54,380
fifteen seconds of video.

36
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:56,631
The first view earns real attention.

37
00:01:56,631 --> 00:02:01,572
The fifth or sixth view from the same person is mostly being ignored, or worse,

38
00:02:01,572 --> 00:02:06,576
actively tuned out, and frequency is the number that tells you how many of those

39
00:02:06,576 --> 00:02:08,827
repeat views have actually piled up.

40
00:02:08,827 --> 00:02:14,081
This is the same mechanic across both platforms from Section five, even if the exact

41
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:17,521
dashboard column isn't labelled identically everywhere.

42
00:02:17,521 --> 00:02:22,712
Whatever a given platform calls the number, you're watching for the same underlying

43
00:02:22,712 --> 00:02:27,215
drift: the same audience seeing the same fifteen seconds too many times.

44
00:02:27,215 --> 00:02:29,216
The label on the column changes.

45
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:34,470
The audience running out of patience for a cut it's already seen five times doesn't.

46
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:38,781
Here's the exact same winning creative, read again weeks later.

47
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:43,676
Frequency past three point four, click-through down to one percent, nearly back to

48
00:02:43,676 --> 00:02:46,602
the pre-testing baseline it started so far above.

49
00:02:46,602 --> 00:02:49,766
Notice what didn't happen: nobody killed this ad set.

50
00:02:49,766 --> 00:02:54,780
The angle itself was never the problem, the same audience had simply seen this exact

51
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:58,183
cut enough times that it stopped earning fresh attention.

52
00:02:58,183 --> 00:03:03,019
The response was a new cut of the same angle, not a new angle entirely, sent back

53
00:03:03,019 --> 00:03:07,914
through the same isolated testing structure from Section seven to confirm it still

54
00:03:07,914 --> 00:03:08,272
works.

55
00:03:08,272 --> 00:03:13,048
Notice, too, that the new cut goes back through testing rather than launching on

56
00:03:13,048 --> 00:03:13,406
faith.

57
00:03:13,406 --> 00:03:18,242
A refresh isn't automatically going to perform, just because the underlying angle

58
00:03:18,242 --> 00:03:18,958
worked once.

59
00:03:18,958 --> 00:03:23,436
It earns its own winner verdict from Section 8.2's tree, on its own merits.

60
00:03:23,436 --> 00:03:26,481
What actually changes in a refresh cut, concretely?

61
00:03:26,481 --> 00:03:31,376
Not the claim, and not the angle: that already proved itself, and re-litigating it

62
00:03:31,376 --> 00:03:34,062
would waste the one thing this winner earned.

63
00:03:34,062 --> 00:03:38,958
What changes is the surface, a different hook, a different pace, a different actor

64
00:03:38,958 --> 00:03:43,077
reading the same underlying script from Section 4.5's assembly stage.

65
00:03:43,077 --> 00:03:45,584
The audience needs a reason to look again.

66
00:03:45,584 --> 00:03:47,733
The argument doesn't need to change.

67
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:52,734
One honest habit worth building before your own first winner goes tired.

68
00:03:52,734 --> 00:03:55,145
Fatigue doesn't arrive without warning.

69
00:03:55,145 --> 00:03:59,534
Frequency climbs steadily, visibly, before performance actually cracks.

70
00:03:59,534 --> 00:04:04,417
Watching frequency specifically, on every ad set currently winning, catches the

71
00:04:04,417 --> 00:04:05,159
trend early.

72
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:10,042
Waiting for click-through to confirm it means you've already spent several days

73
00:04:10,042 --> 00:04:15,235
paying a fatigued creative's higher cost per acquisition before you noticed anything

74
00:04:15,235 --> 00:04:15,853
was wrong.

75
00:04:15,853 --> 00:04:20,984
Build this into the same weekly habit Section 7.3's naming system already asked you

76
00:04:20,984 --> 00:04:21,478
to keep.

77
00:04:21,478 --> 00:04:26,671
A watchlist is only useful if you actually look at it on a schedule, not just when a

78
00:04:26,671 --> 00:04:28,711
number happens to catch your eye.

79
00:04:28,711 --> 00:04:33,594
Check frequency on every current winner once a week, the same discipline you're

80
00:04:33,594 --> 00:04:35,943
already applying to spend and results.

81
00:04:35,943 --> 00:04:38,910
There's a mirror-image mistake worth naming too.

82
00:04:38,910 --> 00:04:43,855
Don't refresh a winner that isn't actually tired yet, just because its frequency

83
00:04:43,855 --> 00:04:45,030
ticked up slightly.

84
00:04:45,030 --> 00:04:48,121
A small rise is normal as an ad set keeps running.

85
00:04:48,121 --> 00:04:53,313
Refreshing reflexively at the first tick wastes production effort on a creative that

86
00:04:53,313 --> 00:04:58,444
was still earning its keep, and it's the same impatience Section 7.4 already warned

87
00:04:58,444 --> 00:05:01,102
you against, aimed at a different decision.

88
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:06,171
Watch for the pairing this table describes, climbing frequency alongside genuinely

89
00:05:06,171 --> 00:05:09,076
declining performance, not either signal alone.

90
00:05:09,626 --> 00:05:14,455
Next lecture answers the question this one leaves open: when a winner does go tired,

91
00:05:14,455 --> 00:05:19,111
do you refresh the exact same angle, or is it time to try something genuinely new

92
00:05:19,111 --> 00:05:19,571
instead.

93
00:05:19,571 --> 00:05:22,445
Worth sitting with one thing before you get there.

94
00:05:22,445 --> 00:05:24,802
Fatigue isn't a sign anything went wrong.

95
00:05:24,802 --> 00:05:29,516
It's what success looks like after enough time has passed, because the audience it

96
00:05:29,516 --> 00:05:34,229
worked on is a fixed, finite group of people, and no creative reaches every one of

97
00:05:34,229 --> 00:05:36,126
them with the same force forever.

98
00:05:36,126 --> 00:05:40,610
Treating a tired winner as a maintenance cost, something you plan for from the

99
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:45,266
moment an angle first wins, is a different posture than treating it as a surprise

100
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:46,416
failure to react to.

101
00:05:46,416 --> 00:05:49,175
One of those keeps the account running smoothly.

102
00:05:49,175 --> 00:05:54,004
The other has you scrambling every few weeks, wondering why a proven winner suddenly

103
00:05:54,004 --> 00:05:58,545
stopped working, when the honest answer was sitting in the frequency column the

104
00:05:58,545 --> 00:05:59,177
whole time.
